{"id":"passwords","title":"Passwords and two-step verification","subject":"Cybersecurity","label":"Cybersecurity","exams":[],"language":"en","seconds":168,"duration":168.47,"audio":"voice.mp3","words":435,"sections":[{"id":"hook","title":"Everyday puzzle","start":0,"end":16.595},{"id":"explain","title":"Why passwords fail","start":16.595,"end":48.235},{"id":"explain2","title":"Second proof","start":48.235,"end":79.015},{"id":"example","title":"Worked example","start":79.015,"end":104.335},{"id":"check","title":"Quick check","start":104.335,"end":115.255},{"id":"answer","title":"The answer","start":115.255,"end":123.065},{"id":"mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","start":123.065,"end":148.695},{"id":"recap","title":"Recap and tip","start":148.695,"end":168.47}],"lines":[{"s":0,"a":0.5,"b":1.11,"text":"I'm Kora.","w":[0.56,0.72]},{"s":0,"a":1.41,"b":5.73,"text":"Have you ever forgotten a password, then reused an old one just to save time?","w":[1.44,1.6,1.8,2.02,2.44,2.52,3.46,3.74,4.2,4.36,4.6,4.78,5,5.12,5.38]},{"s":0,"a":6.18,"b":8.63,"text":"Most people have more accounts than they can remember.","w":[6.26,6.56,6.9,7.14,7.36,7.78,7.92,8.04,8.2]},{"s":0,"a":8.93,"b":16.17,"text":"Today we answer a simple question: why do some passwords fail so quickly, and what makes an account much harder to break into?","w":[8.98,9.38,9.6,9.9,10.02,10.36,11.32,11.62,11.76,11.96,12.52,12.78,13,13.78,13.94,14.12,14.36,14.44,14.92,15.18,15.5,15.6,15.84]},{"s":1,"a":17.02,"b":19.48,"text":"Passwords fail for three common reasons.","w":[17.08,17.68,18,18.2,18.54,18.92]},{"s":1,"a":19.78,"b":24.02,"text":"Short passwords are guessed quickly by programs that try billions of combinations.","w":[19.88,20.16,20.66,20.78,21.1,21.48,21.68,22.18,22.38,22.74,23.18,23.28]},{"s":1,"a":24.32,"b":27.48,"text":"Common passwords sit on lists that attackers already have.","w":[24.38,24.76,25.34,25.6,25.76,26.12,26.3,26.86,27.2]},{"s":1,"a":27.78,"b":31.61,"text":"And a reused password opens every account when one site leaks.","w":[27.8,27.94,28.04,28.58,29.18,29.64,29.92,30.38,30.66,30.86,31.14]},{"s":1,"a":31.91,"b":34.71,"text":"Attackers then try that stolen password elsewhere.","w":[31.96,32.8,33.04,33.32,33.52,33.9,34.34]},{"s":1,"a":35.01,"b":37.18,"text":"This idea is called credential stuffing.","w":[35.06,35.36,35.82,35.94,36.22,36.76]},{"s":1,"a":37.48,"b":39.62,"text":"A strong password is long and unique.","w":[37.54,37.62,37.98,38.52,38.7,39.02,39.18]},{"s":1,"a":39.92,"b":41.27,"text":"Length matters most.","w":[40,40.4,40.8]},{"s":1,"a":41.57,"b":47.81,"text":"A passphrase of four or more random words is long and memorable, and you use a different one for every account.","w":[41.64,41.74,42.44,42.6,43.02,43.14,43.4,43.8,44.24,44.4,44.76,44.9,45.78,45.9,46.12,46.32,46.4,46.8,46.98,47.24,47.5]},{"s":2,"a":48.66,"b":54.03,"text":"Two step verification, also called two factor authentication, adds a second proof.","w":[48.7,48.96,49.22,50.38,50.72,51.16,51.42,51.82,53.04,53.3,53.42,53.76]},{"s":2,"a":54.33,"b":60.6,"text":"It combines something you know, like your password, with something you have, such as a code from an authenticator app.","w":[54.42,54.54,55.1,55.46,55.62,56.08,56.26,56.44,57.38,57.6,57.96,58.12,58.68,58.88,59.02,59.12,59.44,59.62,59.74,60.46]},{"s":2,"a":60.9,"b":63.29,"text":"An app is safer than text message codes.","w":[60.94,61.22,61.46,61.6,61.94,62.14,62.46,62.8]},{"s":2,"a":63.59,"b":65.64,"text":"Never share that code with anyone.","w":[63.78,64.14,64.42,64.64,64.94,65.26]},{"s":2,"a":65.94,"b":71.76,"text":"A password manager remembers a different strong password for every site, behind one strong master password.","w":[66.02,66.1,66.56,66.96,67.46,67.56,67.9,68.26,68.74,68.94,69.2,69.96,70.42,70.62,70.94,71.28]},{"s":2,"a":72.06,"b":75.92,"text":"It fills in passwords for you, so you do not need to remember each one.","w":[72.12,72.24,72.56,72.7,73.24,73.4,74.02,74.18,74.32,74.48,74.66,74.86,74.98,75.42,75.7]},{"s":2,"a":76.22,"b":78.59,"text":"Your master password must be long and private.","w":[76.24,76.46,76.86,77.3,77.5,77.68,77.98,78.12]},{"s":3,"a":79.44,"b":85.58,"text":"Compare an eight character password with mixed letters, numbers and symbols, and a passphrase of four random words.","w":[79.5,79.94,80.1,80.3,80.74,81.28,81.52,81.8,82.28,82.64,82.76,83.56,83.68,83.78,84.38,84.52,84.78,85.12]},{"s":3,"a":85.88,"b":89.11,"text":"The eight character mix looks tricky, but it is short.","w":[85.88,86.1,86.34,86.82,87.12,87.4,88.24,88.4,88.52,88.68]},{"s":3,"a":89.41,"b":92.1,"text":"The four random word passphrase is much longer.","w":[89.5,89.68,90.02,90.4,90.68,91.34,91.48,91.74]},{"s":3,"a":92.4,"b":98.1,"text":"Each extra character multiplies the possible combinations, so length makes guessing far harder.","w":[92.44,92.82,93.12,93.56,94.24,94.36,94.82,96.06,96.38,96.76,97.02,97.46,97.74]},{"s":3,"a":98.4,"b":101.5,"text":"The words should be random, not a famous phrase.","w":[98.46,98.62,98.96,99.18,99.34,100.28,100.5,100.6,100.98]},{"s":3,"a":101.8,"b":103.91,"text":"That is why the longer passphrase wins.","w":[101.82,102.04,102.14,102.34,102.52,102.9,103.44]},{"s":4,"a":104.76,"b":105.44,"text":"Quick check.","w":[104.8,105.08]},{"s":4,"a":105.74,"b":111.92,"text":"You use one strong password on every account, and one site leaks it. What is the risk, and the fix?","w":[105.76,106.02,106.36,106.56,106.92,107.44,107.64,107.9,108.58,108.82,109.02,109.3,109.6,110.2,110.38,110.48,110.6,111.3,111.4,111.52]},{"s":4,"a":112.37,"b":114.83,"text":"Pause here and try it. I'll wait.","w":[112.38,112.76,113.12,113.26,113.56,114.28,114.46]},{"s":5,"a":115.68,"b":122.64,"text":"Every account is now exposed, because attackers try the leaked password elsewhere. Give each account its own password.","w":[115.76,116.06,116.5,116.64,116.88,117.78,118.06,118.62,118.94,119.06,119.38,119.92,120.94,121.18,121.36,121.78,121.98,122.14]},{"s":6,"a":123.49,"b":124.87,"text":"Three common mistakes.","w":[123.58,123.88,124.22]},{"s":6,"a":125.17,"b":129.8,"text":"First, reusing passwords. Avoid it by giving every account its own password.","w":[125.24,125.74,126.26,127.34,127.66,127.76,127.94,128.28,128.54,128.98,129.18,129.34]},{"s":6,"a":130.1,"b":136.34,"text":"Second, sharing a verification code. Never share it, even with someone who says they are support.","w":[130.2,131.08,131.46,131.56,132.3,133.18,133.54,133.82,134.4,134.62,134.82,135.12,135.28,135.54,135.7,135.82]},{"s":6,"a":136.64,"b":141.73,"text":"Third, relying on a short, clever password. Use a long passphrase instead.","w":[136.72,137.5,138,138.1,138.24,138.84,139.18,140.1,140.28,140.36,140.66,141.32]},{"s":6,"a":142.03,"b":148.27,"text":"Also remember, touching systems without permission is illegal. Permission matters, even for a harmless test.","w":[142.1,142.42,143.24,143.66,144.08,144.42,144.96,145.1,146.04,146.48,146.96,147.2,147.36,147.46,147.86]},{"s":7,"a":149.12,"b":149.66,"text":"Recap.","w":[149.18]},{"s":7,"a":149.96,"b":152.14,"text":"Length matters most for password strength.","w":[150.06,150.46,150.86,151.16,151.32,151.84]},{"s":7,"a":152.44,"b":154.59,"text":"Use a unique password for every account.","w":[152.56,152.72,152.84,153.18,153.66,153.88,154.14]},{"s":7,"a":154.89,"b":157.85,"text":"Turn on two step verification and never share the code.","w":[154.94,155.22,155.38,155.58,155.82,156.78,156.94,157.24,157.46,157.58]},{"s":7,"a":158.15,"b":164.46,"text":"Top tip: let a password manager remember your unique passwords, protected by one strong master password.","w":[158.18,158.48,159.2,159.4,159.46,159.92,160.36,160.8,161.02,161.34,162.26,162.72,163,163.18,163.58,163.96]},{"s":7,"a":164.76,"b":167.87,"text":"Thanks for learning with Knowzone, and stay safe online.","w":[164.78,165.08,165.22,165.52,165.68,166.58,166.74,167.06,167.4]}],"scenes":[{"at":0.25,"heading":"Everyday puzzle","items":[{"at":0.4,"kind":"text","text":"Passwords: why do they fail?"},{"at":1.31,"kind":"text","text":"Forgotten a password? Reused an old one?"},{"at":6.08,"kind":"text","text":"More accounts than we can remember"},{"at":8.83,"kind":"text","text":"Why do passwords fail? What makes accounts harder to break?"}]},{"at":16.77,"heading":"Why passwords fail","items":[{"at":16.92,"kind":"text","text":"Three common reasons passwords fail"},{"at":19.68,"kind":"text","text":"Short passwords guessed quickly by programs"},{"at":24.22,"kind":"text","text":"Common passwords are on attacker lists"},{"at":27.68,"kind":"text","text":"Reused password opens every account after a leak"},{"at":34.91,"kind":"term","text":"Credential stuffing","detail":"Trying a stolen password on other sites"}]},{"at":37.23,"heading":"Strong passwords","items":[{"at":37.38,"kind":"text","text":"Strong password: long and unique"},{"at":39.82,"kind":"text","text":"Length matters most"},{"at":41.47,"kind":"term","text":"Passphrase","detail":"Four or more random words; different for every account"}]},{"at":48.41,"heading":"Two-step verification","items":[{"at":48.56,"kind":"term","text":"Two-step verification","detail":"Also called two-factor authentication"},{"at":54.23,"kind":"text","text":"Something you know + something you have"},{"at":60.8,"kind":"text","text":"App safer than text message codes"},{"at":63.49,"kind":"text","text":"Never share the code with anyone"}]},{"at":65.69,"heading":"Password manager","items":[{"at":65.84,"kind":"term","text":"Password manager","detail":"Remembers a strong unique password for every site"},{"at":71.96,"kind":"text","text":"Fills passwords; no need to remember each"},{"at":76.12,"kind":"text","text":"Master password: long and private"}]},{"at":79.19,"heading":"Worked example","items":[{"at":79.34,"kind":"text","text":"Compare: 8-char mix vs 4-word passphrase"},{"at":85.78,"kind":"text","text":"8-char mix looks tricky, but it is short"},{"at":89.31,"kind":"text","text":"4 random words: much longer"},{"at":92.3,"kind":"text","text":"Each extra character multiplies combinations"},{"at":98.3,"kind":"text","text":"Words random, not a famous phrase"}]},{"at":104.33,"heading":"Quick check","items":[{"at":104.33,"kind":"question","text":"You use one strong password on every account, and one site leaks it. What is the risk, and the fix?"}]},{"at":115.43,"heading":"The answer","items":[{"at":115.58,"kind":"text","text":"One leak exposes every account"},{"at":115.58,"kind":"text","text":"Fix: a unique password for each account"}]},{"at":123.24,"heading":"Common mistakes","items":[{"at":123.39,"kind":"text","text":"Three common mistakes"},{"at":125.07,"kind":"wrong","text":"Reusing passwords"},{"at":130,"kind":"wrong","text":"Sharing a verification code"},{"at":136.54,"kind":"wrong","text":"Relying on a short, clever password"},{"at":141.93,"kind":"text","text":"Touching systems without permission is illegal"}]},{"at":148.87,"heading":"Recap and tip","items":[{"at":149.86,"kind":"text","text":"Length matters most for password strength"},{"at":152.34,"kind":"text","text":"Use a unique password for every account"},{"at":154.79,"kind":"text","text":"Turn on two-step verification; never share the code"},{"at":158.05,"kind":"tip","text":"Let a password manager remember unique passwords; one strong master password"}]}],"check":{"at":115.08,"seconds":10,"question":"You use one strong password on every account, and one site leaks it. What is the risk, and the fix?","answer":"Every account is exposed: attackers try the leaked password elsewhere. Give each account its own password."},"fps":30,"mouth":"","level":"","credits":[],"voice":"qwen3-tts-12hz-1.7b (cloned voice)"}